      "source": [
        "### Backprop: SABER (arXiv:2608.27963, s13) x later papers up to 2609.04463 (additive back-links; no prior line touched)\n",
        "\n",
        "- **Paper 11 names the failure SABER cannot see: fluent, stable, WRONG verbal arithmetic** - high confidence, wrong circuit. Loading is the complementary gate before exit (mechanism check vs stability check, same last-token site); a joint exit rule (RSS and loading) predicts exactly the confident-ignorance mode both papers circle.\n",
        "- **Same Jaccard family, different objects:** SABER Eq.3 multiset-Jaccard on answer sets vs paper 11 top-1% Jaccard on unit sets - shared similarity utilities, no shared claim.\n",
        "- **App C patching gives SABER a causal exit audit:** patch the exited-early state's circuit units and test whether the answer survives - a mechanism-level check on top of the statistical one.\n",
        "- **How SABER fares into future dev:** the combo stays train-time analysis, not an inference-time exit - attribution needs gradients and SABER's virtue is staying gradient-free. Do not present joint numbers as exit savings.\n",
        "\n",
        "Ref-graph line (independent citation check): zero shared references (38 refs scanned: thinking-length/efficiency literature only). SABER's Eq.3 multiset-Jaccard was developed without citing circuit-overlap work. Adaptable missing edge: cite-and-compare the two Jaccards (same math, answer-sets vs unit-sets) - cheap, looks intractable only because the two literatures do not talk.\n",
        "\n"
